('ded6a31f-bb38-5bf4-ac85-bbc69346ced5', '46412985-675d-56ef-9f4e-560cc3eb638f', 'The speed of the fastest molecule, squared', 'B', false, 1),
('c3e966b5-1bfb-5150-8a54-3bed49a0e74d', '46412985-675d-56ef-9f4e-560cc3eb638f', 'The mean of the squares of the molecular speeds', 'C', true, 2),
('2ec034ee-1578-5e7d-a992-67d87728ba37', '46412985-675d-56ef-9f4e-560cc3eb638f', 'The mean molecular speed', 'D', false, 3),
('b750f741-fdbc-5d89-ac2f-120c86ce81c1', '0deaa71c-cb7e-5f29-9d6b-1c8cdc317ca0', 'So that average quantities, such as the mean square speed, are well defined and steady', 'A', true, 0),
('fbad5ffb-1e34-5b80-88c2-c946a8663305', '0deaa71c-cb7e-5f29-9d6b-1c8cdc317ca0', 'So that the molecules collide inelastically', 'B', false, 1),
('81601bc9-428b-5acd-8fa0-cd77fe96b215', '0deaa71c-cb7e-5f29-9d6b-1c8cdc317ca0', 'So that the gas has a large mass', 'C', false, 2),
('6c2dc073-47e0-5d54-903b-c365804af004', '0deaa71c-cb7e-5f29-9d6b-1c8cdc317ca0', 'So that the molecules stay at rest', 'D', false, 3),
('27d52145-e215-5b32-b4ba-2e0a3d628f0f', 'eb53762c-3951-5ac1-b236-a9a55a6add4d', 'RN_A', 'A', false, 0),
('421bc7a7-88dc-5169-8d5a-e216d419524b', 'eb53762c-3951-5ac1-b236-a9a55a6add4d', 'N_A/R', 'B', false, 1),
('2cec90b3-9fe8-5324-a69b-15e4ef9088e1', 'eb53762c-3951-5ac1-b236-a9a55a6add4d', 'R/T', 'C', false, 2),
('c5da973c-1036-5be3-8de3-9d360a06342e', 'eb53762c-3951-5ac1-b236-a9a55a6add4d', 'R/N_A', 'D', true, 3),
('0585e691-2dc1-5ee6-9024-638113d8c298', 'd69ef8cc-9005-51ee-b781-cb0eae1a2894', 'It becomes four times larger', 'A', false, 0),
('3a858efa-d055-59f4-af2d-3c693cfc2229', 'd69ef8cc-9005-51ee-b781-cb0eae1a2894', 'It doubles', 'B', true, 1),
('a0b6d327-7c77-5be2-8dda-6cd65360a1d1', 'd69ef8cc-9005-51ee-b781-cb0eae1a2894', 'It becomes sixteen times larger', 'C', false, 2),
('2640e04b-76e5-51f3-8c00-beff50348aef', 'd69ef8cc-9005-51ee-b781-cb0eae1a2894', 'It stays the same', 'D', false, 3),
('0dc1cb4d-c1bb-53cf-9654-9b7c2383be80', '1c4445d6-4fd2-5d69-aca2-cf2a0aecc5fe', 'p = F/A', 'A', false, 0),
('d8c9c90c-5fe8-53db-9d59-564d06b67d0c', '1c4445d6-4fd2-5d69-aca2-cf2a0aecc5fe', 'E = mc²', 'B', false, 1),
('eed183c4-d613-5878-972a-f3d34235d7bb', '1c4445d6-4fd2-5d69-aca2-cf2a0aecc5fe', 'pV = NkT', 'C', true, 2),
('78b7ce94-341b-56bc-a742-ee98b7a423fa', '1c4445d6-4fd2-5d69-aca2-cf2a0aecc5fe', 'F = ma', 'D', false, 3),
('2295b309-9acc-5bf1-ade0-3d3047275d17', '53204d06-01ec-5f63-9a97-037a0e0e6ef2', 'It stays the same', 'A', true, 0),
('794db661-8876-5173-a856-24e307443d75', '53204d06-01ec-5f63-9a97-037a0e0e6ef2', 'It increases', 'B', false, 1),
('8ded8ab4-4947-56ef-8985-c300605e8268', '53204d06-01ec-5f63-9a97-037a0e0e6ef2', 'It decreases', 'C', false, 2),
('d185c2b4-c855-52de-84ba-619fbbe4477c', '53204d06-01ec-5f63-9a97-037a0e0e6ef2', 'It doubles', 'D', false, 3),
('20535ac5-f379-5793-8db7-ed4d8df5bfd2', '3713b18e-7c5e-5a98-a516-b31605085f3f', 'They have a greater mean kinetic energy', 'A', false, 0),
('6845c889-99f7-58e8-a2e3-5a94a5c4e13d', '3713b18e-7c5e-5a98-a516-b31605085f3f', 'They are attracted less by gravity', 'B', false, 1),
('229ff5c7-79af-5f7a-b519-a62ad9814c31', '3713b18e-7c5e-5a98-a516-b31605085f3f', 'They collide less often', 'C', false, 2),
('970e6480-c65c-5713-a7cc-56b7e1feb5d6', '3713b18e-7c5e-5a98-a516-b31605085f3f', 'They have the same mean kinetic energy but a smaller mass', 'D', true, 3),
('ae057f47-2d42-5362-abdf-7ab02e64c3e1', '2eedb580-873e-5b30-ad3d-44cfb35d22a9', 'A curve that levels off', 'A', false, 0),
('c40cc50b-23d7-5dd7-963c-6d4c16b1f47c', '2eedb580-873e-5b30-ad3d-44cfb35d22a9', 'A straight line through the origin', 'B', true, 1),
('94dc6209-9653-55b2-b5f1-bcedd16c7fba', '2eedb580-873e-5b30-ad3d-44cfb35d22a9', 'A straight line with a positive intercept', 'C', false, 2),
('0054686b-f6d6-59ca-9608-dd5e3882c392', '2eedb580-873e-5b30-ad3d-44cfb35d22a9', 'A horizontal line', 'D', false, 3),
('b240542b-2765-501e-a234-7fd2ad793d10', 'b49499fe-8799-5bde-8c79-20fd34b0cb1d', 'J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹', 'A', false, 0),
('8d212217-6c8e-5546-aee3-a6ee7fad6ef1', 'b49499fe-8799-5bde-8c79-20fd34b0cb1d', 'J K', 'B', false, 1),
('fd536f6a-64dc-5ce9-80eb-762e762d498a', 'b49499fe-8799-5bde-8c79-20fd34b0cb1d', 'J K⁻¹', 'C', true, 2),
('54a60854-dd26-5245-9a57-9d71716d5f15', 'b49499fe-8799-5bde-8c79-20fd34b0cb1d', 'K J⁻¹', 'D', false, 3),
('ed21ee45-6061-51b8-8c95-29387f1485ef', '6775962d-0fbc-5509-b22d-3e9fc257bbfb', '1/2', 'A', true, 0),
('99e4ccec-a9da-5ba1-8f51-08924a77b09e', '6775962d-0fbc-5509-b22d-3e9fc257bbfb', '1/4', 'B', false, 1),
('76c65d10-3cf8-5c51-a8c3-a790de4a2321', '6775962d-0fbc-5509-b22d-3e9fc257bbfb', '2', 'C', false, 2),
('519bcac5-a572-5cdc-931f-884b3689bd9b', '6775962d-0fbc-5509-b22d-3e9fc257bbfb', '1', 'D', false, 3),
('3c0a4557-e0f2-5370-93cb-26019965bf04', '7389d7b2-26d0-5ad5-8527-e20eaeb57653', 'Doubling its thermodynamic temperature', 'A', false, 0),
('6eedcfcf-2959-5a8d-871c-2934ad9b808c', '7389d7b2-26d0-5ad5-8527-e20eaeb57653', 'Doubling its pressure at constant temperature', 'B', false, 1),
('b67298fe-4847-5c91-b873-196c44957f73', '7389d7b2-26d0-5ad5-8527-e20eaeb57653', 'Halving its volume at constant temperature', 'C', false, 2),
('9b3cf08d-876a-5447-a986-0f0b29530352', '7389d7b2-26d0-5ad5-8527-e20eaeb57653', 'Increasing its thermodynamic temperature by a factor of 4', 'D', true, 3),
('0533a85f-1d1f-5553-99fb-f626d490d635', '2216045d-0e00-5551-b2ac-d5820d9b61d3', 'The collisions are elastic', 'A', false, 0),
('eb6dbfbb-aa9a-5cab-bd51-d7867eab3f54', '2216045d-0e00-5551-b2ac-d5820d9b61d3', 'The molecules move randomly, with no preferred direction', 'B', true, 1),
('f4fcec4f-2a52-5a36-9912-679b1bf2b6f5', '2216045d-0e00-5551-b2ac-d5820d9b61d3', 'The molecules have negligible volume', 'C', false, 2),
('454da329-057b-5fcd-9105-530d7ad1e5ce', '2216045d-0e00-5551-b2ac-d5820d9b61d3', 'The molecules move in straight lines between collisions', 'D', false, 3),
('dc83d0a2-f18a-5272-90fe-48f60aadff9a', '7683945f-7308-56b0-9a58-1ab24a5684b7', 'The r.m.s. speed is always smaller and easier to measure', 'A', false, 0),
('02e0df75-869a-51f4-804b-3ae100cd83df', '7683945f-7308-56b0-9a58-1ab24a5684b7', 'The mean velocity is infinite', 'B', false, 1),
('45a8a768-eecf-5351-939f-372ac5dd7ba0', '7683945f-7308-56b0-9a58-1ab24a5684b7', 'The mean velocity is zero, because the molecules move randomly in all directions', 'C', true, 2),
('88fcd893-78d8-50e3-97e1-9c105391c879', '7683945f-7308-56b0-9a58-1ab24a5684b7', 'Molecules do not have velocities, only speeds', 'D', false, 3),
('88242212-ab8e-53bb-92ec-556c61374605', '4c85dbd8-96b4-5c6e-9ecd-cae1480f51ce', 'Pressure doubles; r.m.s. speed is unchanged', 'A', true, 0),
('16dbb5d7-df71-5232-b124-61e3ffddc2eb', '4c85dbd8-96b4-5c6e-9ecd-cae1480f51ce', 'Pressure doubles; r.m.s. speed doubles', 'B', false, 1),
('662774ae-4e6c-5d8d-adfd-d748c977a9a9', '4c85dbd8-96b4-5c6e-9ecd-cae1480f51ce', 'Pressure is unchanged; r.m.s. speed doubles', 'C', false, 2),
('b7500f18-9628-572a-952c-2772d8ea7308', '4c85dbd8-96b4-5c6e-9ecd-cae1480f51ce', 'Pressure quadruples; r.m.s. speed is unchanged', 'D', false, 3)
INSERT INTO problems (id, chapter_id, topic_id, curriculum_id, topic_code, syllabus_cite, problem_number, "order", question_text, question_image_url, difficulty_level, answer_type, answer_correct, answer_unit, answer_unit_required, answer_tolerance, answer_sign_sensitive, answer_alternates, explanation, points) VALUES
('98e91140-595b-5270-b13d-1a94224701c1', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 7, 7, 'The mean translational kinetic energy of the molecules of an ideal gas is directly proportional to which quantity?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The thermodynamic temperature', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '⟨E_k⟩ = (3/2)kT, with T in kelvin. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (The thermodynamic temperature).', 1),
('f8bd95b7-c510-5ea7-88ac-1a8dd5f7b246', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 8, 8, 'Estimate the mean translational kinetic energy of a molecule of air in the lungs, at a body temperature of 37 °C. Give your answer in units of 10⁻²¹ J. Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '6.417', '10⁻²¹ J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = 310 K. ⟨E_k⟩ = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 310 = 6.42 × 10⁻²¹ J. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: (3/2)kT with T in kelvin. A1: ⟨E_k⟩ = 6.42 × 10⁻²¹ J.', 2),
('d30a572f-327c-5e58-b2c5-27ce34d17888', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 9, 9, 'Nitrogen gas just above its boiling point is at 77 K. Estimate the mean translational kinetic energy per molecule, in units of 10⁻²¹ J. Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.594', '10⁻²¹ J', false, NULL, false, NULL, '⟨E_k⟩ = (3/2)kT = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 77 = 1.59 × 10⁻²¹ J. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: ⟨E_k⟩ = (3/2)kT. A1: ⟨E_k⟩ = 1.59 × 10⁻²¹ J.', 2),
('2542e180-c8de-5d04-911c-11ecc273ba23', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 10, 10, 'According to the kinetic model of an ideal gas, what is the mean kinetic energy of the molecules at absolute zero?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Zero', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '⟨E_k⟩ = (3/2)kT, which is zero when T = 0 K. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: A (Zero).', 1),
('abbf2bc3-017c-5fbf-871b-7177f3d6d4c8', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 11, 11, 'Calculate the temperature at which the mean translational kinetic energy of gas molecules would be 1.0 eV (1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J). Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '7729', 'K', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = 2⟨E_k⟩/3k = 2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ (3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³) = 7730 K. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: rearranged (3/2)kT. A1: T = 7730 K.', 2),
('2ac6432d-4f52-5c43-84e5-1c70c93acc8b', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 12, 12, 'The thermodynamic temperature of a gas is doubled. What happens to the mean translational kinetic energy of its molecules?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'It doubles', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '⟨E_k⟩ = (3/2)kT, so the mean kinetic energy is proportional to T and doubles with it. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: D (It doubles).', 1),
('06b4e090-7d49-563c-aa24-019193d24c18', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 13, 13, 'Calculate the total translational kinetic energy of the atoms in 2.0 mol of helium at 300 K. Take R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '7479', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = (3/2)nRT = 1.5 × 2.0 × 8.31 × 300 = 7480 J. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: E = (3/2)nRT. A1: E = 7480 J.', 2),
('543069e2-d64b-5576-9e78-a59ae4183500', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 14, 14, 'A gas is warmed from 27 °C to 54 °C. What happens to the mean translational kinetic energy of its molecules?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'It increases by about 9 %', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T rises from 300 K to 327 K: a factor of 1.09. Doubling the Celsius value does not double the energy. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: B (It increases by about 9 %).', 1),
('8dadfadf-ce20-52d3-bbe9-6f5f2b92269c', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 15, 15, 'At 20 °C the molecules of a gas have a certain mean translational kinetic energy. At what thermodynamic temperature would this mean kinetic energy be doubled?', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '586', 'K', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T₁ = 293 K, and ⟨E_k⟩ ∝ T, so T₂ = 2 × 293 = 586 K. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: T₁ in kelvin. A1: T = 586 K.', 2),
('e8b4e77c-587d-5951-9ac9-61e589333987', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 16, 16, 'Nitrogen molecules and carbon dioxide molecules in a room are at the same temperature. Which molecules have the larger r.m.s. speed?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Nitrogen, because the molecules have less mass and the same mean kinetic energy', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Equal (3/2)kT means ½m⟨c²⟩ is the same, so the lighter nitrogen molecules move faster. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (Nitrogen, because the molecules have less mass and the same mean kinetic energy).', 1),
('761f30c0-ed67-5ce3-a455-031c1f263293', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 17, 17, 'A gas is heated from 200 K to 600 K. By what factor does the mean translational kinetic energy of its molecules increase?', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '⟨E_k⟩ ∝ T: factor = 600 ÷ 200 = 3. Mark scheme (1 mark). A1: factor = 3.', 1),
('f57eef85-28af-521d-9f8c-93b9cc280019', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 18, 18, 'The gas at the visible surface of the Sun is at about 5800 K. Calculate the mean translational kinetic energy of a particle there, in eV (1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J). Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.7504', 'eV', false, NULL, false, NULL, '⟨E_k⟩ = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 5800 = 1.20 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. In eV: 1.201 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 0.750. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: (3/2)kT then ÷ e. A1: ⟨E_k⟩ = 0.750 eV.', 2),
('2b7e6e27-d1b9-5f6d-9b0d-94a142c22092', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 19, 19, 'Two gases are placed in thermal contact and reach thermal equilibrium. What can be said about their molecules?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'They have the same mean translational kinetic energy', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Same temperature means the same (3/2)kT per molecule, so there is no net energy transfer between them. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: A (They have the same mean translational kinetic energy).', 1),
('71701897-1dfe-5132-af25-a7d73993d940', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 20, 20, 'The atoms of a gas have an average translational kinetic energy of 1.0 × 10⁻²⁰ J. What is the thermodynamic temperature of the gas? Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '483.1', 'K', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = 2 × 1.0 × 10⁻²⁰ ÷ (3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³) = 483 K. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: T = 2⟨E_k⟩/3k. A1: T = 483 K.', 2),
('7f4a7bd7-803e-5c77-ba6d-3eda5585a331', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 21, 21, 'One mole of helium and one mole of oxygen are at the same temperature. Which quantity is the same for both samples?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The total translational kinetic energy of the molecules', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Each has N_A molecules with (3/2)kT each, so the total (3/2)RT is the same. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: D (The total translational kinetic energy of the molecules).', 1),
('c073c0ea-d13d-5a49-a028-f8b6faa0e8c9', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 22, 22, 'Calculate the r.m.s. speed of neon atoms (mass 3.35 × 10⁻²⁶ kg) at 300 K. Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '608.9', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'c_rms = √(3kT/m) = √(3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 ÷ 3.35 × 10⁻²⁶) = 609 m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: ½m⟨c²⟩ = (3/2)kT. A1: c_rms = 609 m s⁻¹.', 2),
('1ef7f637-7324-5224-819b-b513cb406b96', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 23, 23, 'Which expression gives the mean translational kinetic energy of one molecule of an ideal gas at thermodynamic temperature T?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, '(3/2)kT', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(3/2)RT is the energy per mole; per molecule it is (3/2)kT. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: B ((3/2)kT).', 1),
('a14bbfc5-bd94-53a5-af62-70856d53a0a9', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 24, 24, 'Calculate the temperature at which oxygen molecules (mass 5.31 × 10⁻²⁶ kg) would have an r.m.s. speed of 1000 m s⁻¹. Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1283', 'K', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = m⟨c²⟩/3k = 5.31 × 10⁻²⁶ × 1000² ÷ (3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³) = 1280 K. Mark scheme (3 marks). M1: ½m⟨c²⟩ = (3/2)kT. C1: rearrangement. A1: T = 1280 K.', 3),
('712cfe6f-8c5f-5e1b-bdc6-2567fde42388', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 25, 25, 'Helium is cooled from 127 °C to −73 °C. Calculate the ratio (r.m.s. speed at 127 °C) ÷ (r.m.s. speed at −73 °C).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.414', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T₁ = 400 K and T₂ = 200 K. c ∝ √T, so ratio = √(400 ÷ 200) = 1.41. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: temperatures in kelvin. C1: c ∝ √T. A1: ratio = 1.41.', 3),
('12b557bb-ecad-526f-bb36-6e8af37ef7c8', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 26, 26, 'A sample of 0.50 mol of an ideal monatomic gas is heated in a sealed rigid container from 300 K to 500 K. Calculate the increase in the internal energy of the gas. Take R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1246', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'For an ideal monatomic gas the internal energy is all translational kinetic energy: ΔU = (3/2)nRΔT = 1.5 × 0.50 × 8.31 × 200 = 1250 J. Mark scheme (4 marks). B1: internal energy = total kinetic energy. C1: ΔU = (3/2)nRΔT. C1: ΔT = 200 K. A1: ΔU = 1250 J.', 4),
('467a8909-98e5-5d59-86f4-b857ac1c7ecc', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 27, 27, 'What is the mean translational kinetic energy of the molecules of a gas at 0 °C?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'About 5.7 × 10⁻²¹ J', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(3/2)kT = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 273 = 5.65 × 10⁻²¹ J. Zero would require 0 K, not 0 °C. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: T = 273 K. C1: (3/2)kT. A1: C (About 5.7 × 10⁻²¹ J).', 3),
('a97cf02f-80ca-5cf9-aad0-14a166bfd037', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 28, 28, 'A room of volume 60 m³ is filled with air at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. Calculate the total translational kinetic energy of the air molecules.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '9e6', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'From pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩, total E_k = (3/2)pV = 1.5 × 1.0 × 10⁵ × 60 = 9.0 × 10⁶ J. Mark scheme (3 marks). M1: E = (3/2)pV. C1: substitution. A1: E = 9.0 × 10⁶ J.', 3),
('59975148-c115-5b63-9b02-037a37493632', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 29, 29, 'A gas at a pressure of 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa contains 2.5 × 10²⁵ molecules per cubic metre. Calculate the mean translational kinetic energy of a molecule, in units of 10⁻²¹ J.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '6', '10⁻²¹ J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'p = nkT, so kT = p/n and ⟨E_k⟩ = (3/2)p/n = 1.5 × 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ 2.5 × 10²⁵ = 6.00 × 10⁻²¹ J. Mark scheme (3 marks). M1: ⟨E_k⟩ = (3/2)p/n. C1: substitution. A1: ⟨E_k⟩ = 6.00 × 10⁻²¹ J.', 3),
('0dd1dd90-0132-54ea-b942-17a54add33b2', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 30, 30, 'A mixture of hydrogen (molar mass 2 g mol⁻¹) and oxygen (molar mass 32 g mol⁻¹) is in thermal equilibrium. Which row gives the ratios for hydrogen ÷ oxygen?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Mean kinetic energy ratio 1; r.m.s. speed ratio 4', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Same T gives equal ⟨E_k⟩, so c² ∝ 1/m and the speed ratio is √16 = 4. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: equal mean kinetic energy. C1: speed ratio √(32/2). A1: A (Mean kinetic energy ratio 1; r.m.s. speed ratio 4).', 3),
('6b7c9cc2-4d3f-53ce-966c-686d9e65717b', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 31, 31, 'A sealed rigid container holds 2.0 mol of argon. Calculate the energy that must be supplied to heat the argon from 20 °C to 80 °C, assuming the container itself absorbs no energy. Take R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1496', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'No work is done at constant volume, so all the energy becomes translational kinetic energy: Q = (3/2)nRΔT = 1.5 × 2.0 × 8.31 × 60 = 1500 J. Mark scheme (4 marks). B1: no work done at constant volume. C1: Q = ΔU = (3/2)nRΔT. C1: ΔT = 60 K. A1: Q = 1500 J.', 4),
('cbc3a23f-8ef0-5626-aba7-5f16e5c76582', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 32, 32, 'In a fusion experiment, the ions in a plasma have a mean kinetic energy of 10 keV (1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J). Calculate the temperature of the plasma. Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '7.729e7', 'K', false, NULL, false, NULL, '⟨E_k⟩ = 10 × 10³ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ J. T = 2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ ÷ (3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³) = 7.73 × 10⁷ K. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: keV → J. C1: T = 2⟨E_k⟩/3k. A1: T = 7.73 × 10⁷ K.', 3),
('4a85dbc6-cb29-5086-8608-32c84a543578', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 33, 33, 'A gas in a cylinder expands rapidly, pushing a piston outwards, and no energy is supplied by heating. Why does the temperature of the gas fall?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Molecules rebound from the moving piston with less speed, so the mean kinetic energy, and hence the temperature, decreases', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The gas does work on the piston; that energy comes from the molecules'' kinetic energy, and T ∝ ⟨E_k⟩. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: gas does work on piston. C1: molecules lose kinetic energy. C1: T ∝ mean kinetic energy. A1: D (Molecules rebound from the moving piston with less speed, so the mean kinetic energy, and hence the temperature, decreases).', 4),
('a849206f-e881-5dab-8224-cc6162366f97', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 34, 34, 'Calculate the r.m.s. speed of water-vapour molecules (molar mass 0.018 kg mol⁻¹) at 100 °C. Take R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '718.8', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'c_rms = √(3RT/M) = √(3 × 8.31 × 373 ÷ 0.018) = 719 m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: T = 373 K. C1: c_rms = √(3RT/M). A1: c_rms = 719 m s⁻¹.', 3),
('43542696-e7cf-5c62-94a7-db0f2884ce11', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 35, 35, 'The escape speed from the Earth is 11.2 km s⁻¹. Calculate the temperature at which nitrogen molecules (mass 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶ kg) would have this r.m.s. speed. Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.409e5', 'K', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = m⟨c²⟩/3k = 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶ × 11 200² ÷ (3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³) = 1.41 × 10⁵ K. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: speed in m s⁻¹. C1: T = m⟨c²⟩/3k. A1: T = 1.41 × 10⁵ K.', 3),
('b31929ff-ed40-5030-94f7-565a98b0e8d2', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 36, 36, 'Why is (3/2)kT described as the mean *translational* kinetic energy of a molecule?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'It counts only the motion of each molecule''s centre of mass, not its rotation or vibration', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Diatomic molecules can also rotate and vibrate; (3/2)kT is the share in motion through space. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: centre-of-mass motion. C1: excludes rotation and vibration. A1: B (It counts only the motion of each molecule''s centre of mass, not its rotation or vibration).', 3),
('3d7c9bc5-2ca5-5b7f-bd8b-ab04a57a2765', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 37, 37, 'In a simulation, gas molecules at 360 K have a mean translational kinetic energy of 7.45 × 10⁻²¹ J. Use these values to calculate the Boltzmann constant, in units of 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.38', '10⁻²³ J K⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'k = 2⟨E_k⟩/3T = 2 × 7.45 × 10⁻²¹ ÷ (3 × 360) = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹. Mark scheme (4 marks). M1: ⟨E_k⟩ = (3/2)kT. C1: rearranged for k. C1: substitution. A1: k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', 4),
('e6b1b80a-ff07-5727-abc6-3d46117554f8', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 38, 38, 'Calculate the ratio of the internal energy of 1.0 mol of helium at 400 K to the internal energy of 3.0 mol of neon at 200 K. Treat both as ideal monatomic gases.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.6667', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'U = (3/2)nRT for each, so the ratio = (1.0 × 400) ÷ (3.0 × 200) = 0.667. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: U ∝ nT. C1: substitution. A1: ratio = 0.667.', 3),
('fd0ff84f-21fa-522d-ac15-863ec125d9c3', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 39, 39, 'A gas is compressed slowly while its temperature is kept constant. What happens to the mean kinetic energy of its molecules, and why?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'It is unchanged, because the temperature is unchanged; the work done on the gas is transferred out by heating', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '⟨E_k⟩ depends only on T. For a constant-temperature compression, energy equal to the work done leaves the gas as heat. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: ⟨E_k⟩ depends on T only. C1: energy leaves by heating. A1: C (It is unchanged, because the temperature is unchanged; the work done on the gas is transferred out by heating).', 3),
('a4b65fd1-b77a-5031-9efa-6be281d6c7bd', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 40, 40, 'In a cold-storage room the air molecules have an average translational kinetic energy of 4.9 × 10⁻²¹ J. What is the Celsius temperature of the room? Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '-36.29', '°C', false, NULL, true, NULL, 'T = 2 × 4.9 × 10⁻²¹ ÷ (3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³) = 237 K. In °C: 236.7 − 273 = −36.3. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: T = 2⟨E_k⟩/3k. C1: conversion to °C. A1: θ = −36.3 °C.', 3),
('00c93263-24dd-5a5f-b308-96c22879962b', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 41, 41, 'The core of the Sun is at about 1.5 × 10⁷ K. (a) Calculate the mean translational kinetic energy of a proton there, in keV (1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J). (b) Calculate the r.m.s. speed of the protons (mass 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg), in km s⁻¹. (c) The energy needed for two protons to come close enough to fuse is far greater than the mean kinetic energy. Suggest why fusion still happens in the core. Give only the answer to (b). Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '609.8', 'km s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) ⟨E_k⟩ = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 1.5 × 10⁷ = 3.10 × 10⁻¹⁶ J. In keV: 3.105 × 10⁻¹⁶ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁶ = 1.94. (b) c_rms = √(3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 1.5 × 10⁷ ÷ 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷) = 6.10 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹. In km s⁻¹: 6.098 × 10⁵ ÷ 1000 = 610. (c) There is a wide distribution of speeds, so a tiny fraction of the enormous number of protons have far more than the mean energy; quantum tunnelling also helps. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: (3/2)kT. C1: conversion to keV. C1: c_rms = √(3kT/m). C1: conversion to km s⁻¹. B1: distribution of speeds / tunnelling. A1: c_rms = 610 km s⁻¹.', 6),
('52baa5b0-896c-5fd3-8c33-aef1da3d2777', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 42, 42, 'The top of the Earth''s atmosphere is at about 1200 K. The escape speed there is about 11.2 km s⁻¹. (a) Calculate the r.m.s. speed of hydrogen atoms (mass 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg) at 1200 K. (b) Calculate the ratio of the escape speed to this r.m.s. speed. (c) Explain why the Earth has lost most of its hydrogen but kept its nitrogen. Give only the answer to (b). Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.053', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) c_rms = √(3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 1200 ÷ 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷) = 5450 m s⁻¹. (b) Ratio = 11 200 ÷ 5454 = 2.05. (c) Hydrogen''s r.m.s. speed is only about half the escape speed, so a significant fraction of atoms in the fast tail escape. Nitrogen molecules are 28 times as massive and move about 5 times more slowly, so almost none reach escape speed. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: c_rms = √(3kT/m). C1: speed ratio. B1: speed distribution tail. B1: nitrogen much slower. A1: ratio = 2.05.', 5),
('ad88aa12-4977-5194-bbd3-6005355c685e', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 43, 43, 'A rigid cylinder of volume 0.010 m³ contains helium at 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa and 300 K. (a) Calculate the amount of helium. (b) Calculate the internal energy of the helium. (c) Calculate the energy that must be supplied to heat the helium to 400 K. (d) Calculate the final pressure. Give only the answer to (c). Take R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1000', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) n = 2.0 × 10⁵ × 0.010 ÷ (8.31 × 300) = 0.802 mol. (b) U = (3/2)nRT = (3/2)pV = 1.5 × 2.0 × 10⁵ × 0.010 = 3000 J. (c) No work is done, so Q = (3/2)nRΔT = 1.5 × 0.8022 × 8.31 × 100 = 1000 J. (d) p ∝ T: p = 2.0 × 10⁵ × 400 ÷ 300 = 2.67 × 10⁵ Pa. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: n = pV/RT. C1: U = (3/2)nRT. M1: Q = ΔU at constant volume. C1: ΔT = 100 K. C1: p ∝ T. A1: Q = 1000 J.', 6),
('0ae4d6b0-7db1-5532-ad4d-b8826132ec54', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 44, 44, 'In an insulated container, 1.0 mol of helium at 300 K is mixed with 2.0 mol of helium at 450 K. No energy is lost to the surroundings. (a) Explain why the total translational kinetic energy of the atoms is conserved. (b) Calculate the final temperature. (c) Calculate the mean translational kinetic energy of an atom after mixing, in units of 10⁻²¹ J. Give only the answer to (b). Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '400', 'K', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) The container is insulated and does no work, and the atoms'' energy is all kinetic, so the total kinetic energy cannot change. (b) (3/2)R(1.0 × 300 + 2.0 × 450) = (3/2)R × 3.0 × T, so T = (1.0 × 300 + 2.0 × 450) ÷ 3.0 = 400 K. (c) ⟨E_k⟩ = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 400 = 8.28 × 10⁻²¹ J. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: insulated, no work, energy all kinetic. M1: total (3/2)nRT conserved. C1: substitution. C1: (3/2)kT at final T. A1: T = 400 K.', 5),
('9804b54c-b1b3-5a17-bca8-23460ba4fdbc', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 45, 45, 'A computer model gives the mean translational kinetic energy ⟨E⟩ of gas molecules: T/K = 100, 200, 300, 400 and ⟨E⟩/10⁻²¹ J = 2.07, 4.14, 6.21, 8.28. (a) Show that ⟨E⟩ is proportional to T. (b) Calculate the gradient of the graph of ⟨E⟩ against T. (c) Use the gradient to find the Boltzmann constant. (d) Hence calculate the molar gas constant, using N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹. Give only the answer to (d), in J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '8.308', 'J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) ⟨E⟩/T = 2.07 × 10⁻²¹ ÷ 100 = 2.07 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹ for every reading, so ⟨E⟩ ∝ T. (b) Gradient = (8.28 − 2.07) × 10⁻²¹ ÷ (400 − 100) = 2.07 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹. (c) Gradient = (3/2)k, so k = 2 × 2.07 × 10⁻²³ ÷ 3 = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹. (d) R = N_A k = 6.02 × 10²³ × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: ⟨E⟩/T constant. C1: gradient. M1: gradient = (3/2)k. C1: k value. C1: R = N_A k. A1: R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.', 6),
('3621fe31-d37c-5688-88c5-7c08fdc36cf7', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 46, 46, 'A balloon contains 0.50 mol of helium at 290 K. Sunlight warms it to 310 K while the pressure stays at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. (a) Calculate the increase in the volume of the balloon. (b) Calculate the increase in the internal energy of the helium. (c) Calculate the work done by the helium as it expands. (d) Use the first law of thermodynamics to calculate the energy supplied by heating. Give only the answer to (d). Take R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '207.8', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) ΔV = nRΔT/p = 0.50 × 8.31 × 20 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁵ = 8.31 × 10⁻⁴ m³. (b) ΔU = (3/2)nRΔT = 1.5 × 0.50 × 8.31 × 20 = 125 J. (c) W = pΔV = 1.0 × 10⁵ × 8.310 × 10⁻⁴ = 83.1 J. (d) Q = ΔU + W = 124.7 + 83.10 = 208 J. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: ΔV = nRΔT/p. C1: ΔU = (3/2)nRΔT. C1: W = pΔV. M1: energy supplied = ΔU + work done by gas. C1: sum. A1: Q = 208 J.', 6),
('f7adab37-6685-5e9a-827e-7f13fe99b4c3', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 47, 47, 'The air in a room warms from 15 °C to 25 °C. (a) Calculate the percentage increase in the mean translational kinetic energy of the molecules. (b) Calculate the percentage increase in their r.m.s. speed. (c) Explain why the two percentages differ. Give only the answer to (b), in %.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.721', '%', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) ⟨E_k⟩ ∝ T: 298 ÷ 288 = 1.035, an increase of 3.47 %. (b) c ∝ √T: √(298 ÷ 288) = 1.0172, so the increase = (1.0172 − 1) × 100 = 1.72 %. (c) Kinetic energy depends on c², so the speed changes by about half the percentage of the energy. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: temperatures in kelvin. C1: energy ratio. C1: c ∝ √T. B1: E ∝ c². A1: increase = 1.72%.', 5),
('97dd5187-f791-580c-9439-67cec0b10021', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 48, 48, 'A sealed container of nitrogen at 290 K is on a spacecraft orbiting at 7.7 km s⁻¹. A nitrogen molecule has mass 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶ kg. (a) Calculate the kinetic energy of one molecule due to the orbital motion of the spacecraft. (b) Calculate the mean translational kinetic energy of a molecule due to its random thermal motion. (c) Calculate the ratio of (a) to (b). (d) Explain why the orbital motion does not affect the temperature of the gas. Give only the answer to (c). Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '229.6', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = ½mv² = 0.5 × 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶ × 7700² = 1.38 × 10⁻¹⁸ J. (b) ⟨E_k⟩ = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 290 = 6.00 × 10⁻²¹ J. (c) Ratio = 1.378 × 10⁻¹⁸ ÷ 6.003 × 10⁻²¹ = 230. (d) Temperature depends only on the random motion of molecules relative to the container; the ordered motion of the whole gas does not change the molecules'' speeds relative to each other. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: ½mv² for orbital motion. C1: (3/2)kT. C1: ratio. B1: temperature measures random motion only. A1: ratio = 230.', 5),
('f136a0f9-8b53-5af6-a564-792ffd5eb0b8', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 49, 49, 'Laser cooling can bring a cloud of rubidium atoms (mass 1.42 × 10⁻²⁵ kg) to a temperature of 100 µK. (a) Calculate the mean translational kinetic energy of an atom at this temperature, in units of 10⁻²⁷ J. (b) Calculate the r.m.s. speed of the atoms. (c) Calculate the ratio of the r.m.s. speed at 300 K to that at 100 µK. (d) Explain why the cloud must be held in a vacuum. Give only the answer to (b). Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.1707', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) ⟨E_k⟩ = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 2.07 × 10⁻²⁷ J. (b) c_rms = √(3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 1.42 × 10⁻²⁵) = 0.171 m s⁻¹. (c) Ratio = √(300 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻⁴) = 1730. (d) Collisions with room-temperature gas molecules would give the atoms energy and heat the cloud. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: (3/2)kT with T = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ K. C1: c_rms = √(3kT/m). C1: substitution. C1: c ∝ √T ratio. B1: collisions would warm the atoms. A1: c_rms = 0.171 m s⁻¹.', 6),
('fe31197a-28c8-5b55-bb08-0da8f2a19414', '468b27d0-3cef-43ee-aeef-09b1a340050f', 'c69befdb-0bb1-4d84-a2b1-56487e6070d7', 'a-level', '20.8', '9702 coursebook 20.8 · Temperature and molecular kinetic energy', 50, 50, 'A room of volume 50 m³ contains air at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa and 290 K. (a) Calculate the amount of air in the room. (b) Calculate the increase in the total translational kinetic energy of the air molecules when the temperature rises by 5.0 K at constant volume. (c) Explain why the energy needed to warm the air in practice is larger than your answer to (b). Give only the answer to (b). Take R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.293e5', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) n = 1.0 × 10⁵ × 50 ÷ (8.31 × 290) = 2070 mol. (b) ΔE = (3/2)nRΔT = 1.5 × 2075 × 8.31 × 5.0 = 1.29 × 10⁵ J. (c) Air molecules are diatomic and also gain rotational kinetic energy, and energy is also lost to the walls and furniture. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: n = pV/RT. C1: ΔE = (3/2)nRΔT. C1: substitution. B1: rotational energy and losses. A1: ΔE = 1.29 × 10⁵ J.', 5)
